---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import G from "../data/graves.json";
// The photographed cemetery surveys — Otočac 8 October 2024, Senj the 9th.
// Read out 15 September 2026 and, until today, published nowhere: graves.py
// read only sources/findagrave/, so the only Croatian evidence in this archive
// sat on disk. The two kinds are kept apart on purpose — an index is somebody
// else's transcription, a stone is a photograph of the thing itself.
const S = G.stones;
const matched = S.rows.filter((r) => r.slug);
const big = S.stones.filter((s) => s.people.length > 2).slice(0, 8);
const conf = Object.fromEntries(S.confidence.map(([k, n]) => [k, n]));
---
<Base title="The Stones"
  eyebrow="Otočac and Senj, photographed October 2024"
  dek="Two hundred and twenty-six names read off photographs of the memorials themselves. Five direct ancestors are among them — and every stone that disagrees with the tree disagrees by one year, on a birth, never on a death.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This archive holds two kinds of cemetery evidence and they are
    <strong>not the same thing</strong>. <a href={u("/port-pirie/")}>Find a Grave</a> gives
    <strong>{G.n} memorials</strong> — an <strong>index</strong>, somebody else's transcription,
    where a date may be the burial rather than the death. These
    <strong>{S.n} names</strong> are <strong>stones</strong>: read off photographs of the memorials
    themselves, at full resolution, in this archive. For Senj and Otočac it is the only evidence
    there is, because <strong>Find a Grave has never covered either</strong>.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      {S.surveys.map((s) => (
        <tr><th>{s.cemetery}</th><td>photographed <strong>{s.photographed}</strong> ·
          <strong>{s.n}</strong> names · <span class="mono dim">{s.file}</span></td></tr>))}
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">How sure each reading is</div>
    <h2>The confidence letter is the whole apparatus</h2>
    <p>A gravestone photographed at a steep angle runs its last words out of frame, and a vase of
    flowers stands exactly where the dates are. So every row says how it was read, and the letters
    are not decoration — <strong>they have already been right four times</strong>.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th class="mono">r</th><td><strong>{conf.r}</strong> rows — cropped and enlarged three to
        eight times and read at full resolution.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">p</th><td><strong>{conf.p}</strong> rows — read at contact-sheet scale.
        The surname is secure; <strong>the digits are not verified</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="mono">?</th><td><strong>{conf["?"]}</strong> rows — uncertain, and not to be
        relied on for anything.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Four dates were wrong, and every one came
    off a contact sheet.</strong> Mile Prpić 1985 → <strong>1982</strong>; Karlo Papić-Glavičić
    1928 → <strong>1922</strong>; Budimir Boras 1950 → <strong>1954</strong>; and a man recorded as
    <em>Pavo</em> Boras who is <strong>Fabo</strong>. Two more turned out to have
    <strong>no readable death year at all</strong> — the years first written down ran off the edge
    of the photograph. <strong>Nothing in the ancestor block moved:</strong> those rows were read at
    full resolution the first time, and every one was confirmed.
    <strong>Only an «r» row is allowed to contradict the tree below.</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{matched.length} of {S.n} are people on this site</div>
    <h2>Who is matched, and how</h2>
    <p class="lede">A Croatian stone gives a married woman under <strong>her husband's
    surname</strong>. «MILKA BLAŽEVIĆ <em>rod.</em> PAPIĆ» is this tree's Milka Papić, and matching
    on the maiden name alone would have missed her, her mother-in-law and her mother-in-law's
    daughter-in-law alike. Three of the matches below are made through a
    <strong>marriage</strong>, and they are marked.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:30%">On the stone</th><th style="width:12%"></th>
        <th>In this tree</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{matched.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{r.given} {r.surname}</strong>
            <span class="mono dim" style="display:block">{r.born || "?"}–{r.died || "?"}</span>
            <span class="dek" style="display:block">{r.cemetery}</span></td>
          <td class="mono">{r.conf}</td>
          <td><a href={u(`/people/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.archiveName}</a>
            <span class="mono dim" style="display:block">{r.archiveBorn || "?"} – {r.archiveDied || "?"}</span>
            {r.viaMarriedName &&
              <span class="dek" style="display:block">matched through her married name</span>}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Three disagreements, and they are one disagreement</div>
    <h2>A stone is good for a death and weak for a birth</h2>
    <p class="lede">Where a full-resolution reading matches somebody here, the years were compared
    rather than merged. <strong>Every difference is a birth year and every difference is exactly one
    year.</strong> Not one death year disagrees anywhere in {S.n} names.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Field</th><th>This tree</th><th>The stone</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{S.conflicts.map((c) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={u(`/people/${c.slug}/`)}>{c.name}</a>
            <span class="dek" style="display:block">{c.cemetery}</span></td>
          <td class="dek">{c.field === "born" ? "birth" : "death"}</td>
          <td class="mono">{c.tree}</td>
          <td class="mono">{c.stone}</td>
          <td class="dek">{c.years > 0 ? `+${c.years}` : c.years} year</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">It is the obvious thing once it is written down. <strong>A death is
    carved by people who have just lived through it.</strong> A birth is carved from memory,
    sometimes forty years later, by the generation after. The pattern holds across
    <strong>two cemeteries and three families</strong> — Blažević and Papić at Senj, Žubrinić at
    Otočac — which is what makes it a rule rather than a coincidence.</p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>The direction is not consistent, and an earlier
    draft of the Senj note said it was.</strong> Ljubomir's stone is a year <em>early</em>; the
    other two are a year <em>late</em>. That claim was written before these surveys were wired into
    this page, and putting the three side by side is what exposed it. <strong>It is the size that
    means something, not the sign.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{S.stones.length} stones carry more than one name</div>
    <h2>Who is in the ground together</h2>
    <p>A plot number is a household claim, and a family stone is the same claim in carved letters.
    These are the largest.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:34%">The stone</th><th>Who is on it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{big.map((s) => (
        <tr>
          <td><span class="dek">{s.cemetery}</span>
            <span style="display:block">{s.stone}</span>
            <span class="mono dim" style="display:block">{s.photos.join(", ")}</span></td>
          <td>{s.people.map((x) => (
            <span style="display:block">
              {x.slug
                ? <a href={u(`/people/${x.slug}/`)}><strong>{x.given} {x.surname}</strong></a>
                : <strong>{x.given} {x.surname}</strong>}
              <span class="mono dim"> {x.born || "?"}–{x.died || "?"}</span>
              <span class="mono dim"> [{x.conf}]</span>
              {!x.slug && <span class="dek"> — not in this tree</span>}
            </span>))}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What is not read</div>
    <h2>The rest wants ten minutes in the cemetery, not another pass</h2>
    <p><strong>{conf.p} rows are «p» and {conf["?"]} are «?»</strong>, and that is now a statement
    about the photographs rather than a backlog. Where a stone was shot at a steep angle the far end
    of each line runs out of frame; where flowers stood on the kerb, they stood on the dates.
    <strong>No amount of enlargement fixes either.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">One name defeated the sweep entirely — a fallen plaque under reeds,
    upside down, reading «<strong>MARIJAN …</strong>» and no more. It is the single best reason to
    go back. <a href={u("/photograph-these/")}>What to photograph →</a></p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And one surname here belongs to another
    archive.</strong> «IVAN DEFRANĆESKI 1925–1995» stands in the same ground and does not occur once
    in this tree. It is recorded because it was photographed, and joined to nothing.</div>
  </section>
</Base>
